{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Rule 6c-11** is available to eligible **open-end management investment company ETFs**—including index, actively managed, leveraged and inverse ETFs—if they meet the rule’s conditions, including daily portfolio-holdings website disclosure, written basket procedures, recordkeeping, and (for custom baskets) designated review.\n\nIt does **not** cover **UIT ETFs** (including many legacy index ETFs), **share-class ETFs**, or **non-transparent/semi-transparent ETFs** that do not disclose full holdings daily; those generally need separate exemptive relief or another applicable basis. **ETNs** are not registered investment-company ETFs and do not use Rule 6c-11.\n\nLeveraged/inverse status alone does not require separate Rule 6c-11 relief. But derivatives can trigger **Investment Company Act Rule 18f-4**: an ETF using derivatives must comply with its derivatives-risk program, VaR limits and reporting, unless it qualifies for the limited-user exception. Rule 18f-4 governs derivatives use; it neither grants Rule 6c-11 relief nor bars an ETF from relying on it."}}
